# Analysis plan: sample-pca

Status: DRAFT

This plan awaits user review. Approval freezes the plan; changes after approval require a new analysis.

## Goal
Produce a PCA of the RNA-seq samples from gene counts, coloured by condition.
Show the six samples (three untreated and three treated) on PC1 versus PC2, with sample labels and explained variance on both axes.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve using `_system/resolve_artifact.py --project <project> --assay rnaseq_bulk --consumes counts_gene design` again at execution time. Paths below are relative to the project and are the current native resolutions.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene | 01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper (native) | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |
| design | 01_prepare_samplesheets (native) | 01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv |

## Method
1. After approval, implement `scripts/sample_pca.py` and `scripts/submit.sh` inside this analysis directory. Run Python in `gars-bio`, using pandas, NumPy, scikit-learn and Matplotlib. Source the workspace's `_system/gars-env.sh` for environment configuration. Check the required imports inside the batch job; if unavailable, stop and report the missing dependency without installing anything.
2. Re-resolve both inputs by type. Read the tab-separated gene matrix and comma-separated design. Use `gene_id` as the identifier and exclude `gene_name` from numeric sample columns. Require unique, nonempty gene and sample identifiers, finite nonnegative counts, positive library totals, and a nonempty condition for each sample. Match sample columns exactly to design sample IDs and order by the design; stop on duplicates or mismatches rather than silently discarding samples. Preserve fractional counts from the native length-scaled matrix.
3. Normalize each sample to counts per million (CPM), using its total across all input genes. Retain genes with CPM >= 1 in at least 3 samples (the size of each condition group here). Transform retained values with log2(CPM + 1). Do not select genes using condition differences or differential expression results.
4. Rank genes by sample variance of transformed values (sample variance, ddof=1), excluding genes with zero variance. Use the top 500 genes, or all remaining genes if fewer than 500; break variance ties by gene_id. Require at least two selected genes and enough nonzero matrix rank for two components, otherwise stop with a clear error.
5. Apply `sklearn.decomposition.PCA(n_components=2, svd_solver="full", whiten=False)` to the samples-by-selected-genes matrix. Center each gene across samples, as PCA does by default, without scaling genes to unit variance. Condition labels are used only to colour the resulting points.
6. Save PC1 versus PC2 as PNG (300 dpi) and PDF, with sample IDs next to points, distinct colours for untreated and treated, a condition legend, and percentage variance explained on each axis. Export sample scores with condition, explained variance and eigenvalues for PC1/PC2, and the selected genes with their transformation variances.
7. Write a brief report recording resolved input paths and supplying stages, input SHA256 hashes, sample and condition counts, genes retained and selected, normalization/filter/PCA parameters, package versions and explained variance. Describe the plot as exploratory; do not perform significance tests, remove outliers, correct batch effects, or infer treatment effects. Keep all inputs read-only and write scripts, logs and results only within this analysis directory.
8. After successful execution, run `stage03_analysis.py verify` to check declared outputs and register completion through the stage's standard mechanism.

